<activity xml:id="act-ConvertingPoint">
    <title>Converting the Coordinates of a Point</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            We're going to think about the two coordinate systems (and converting between them) by considering a point and collecting information. For simplicity, we'll visualize a point in the first quadrant.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-point">
            <caption></caption>
            <image source="./ParametricPolar/point.png" width="50%">
                <shortdescription>A point in the first quadrant. There is a line connecting the origin with the point, and the horizontal and vertical components are shown as dashed lines, making a right triangle. The angle formed by the horizontal axis and the line to the point is labeled.</shortdescription>
            </image>
        </figure>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If the point is written in Cartesian coordinates, <m>(x,y)</m>, label <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> in the image.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If the point is written in polar coordinates, <m>(r,\theta)</m>, label <m>\theta</m> and <m>r</m> in the image.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Assume that we know the values of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>. Come up with an equation linking <m>r</m>, <m>x</m>, and <m>y</m> in order for us to find the value of <m>r</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                What equations do we have connecting the side lengths of a triangle like this one?
            </p>
        </hint>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Now come up with some equation linking <m>\theta</m>, <m>x</m>, and <m>y</m> in order for us to find the value of <m>\theta</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                What kinds of trigonometric functions connect these three variables?
            </p>
        </hint>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Now imagine that we know the values of <m>r</m> and <m>\theta</m> instead. Come up with an equation connecting <m>x</m>, <m>r</m>, and <m>\theta</m> in order for us to find the value of <m>x</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                What kinds of trigonometric functions connect these two side lengths of this triangle?
            </p>
        </hint>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Come up with an equation connecting <m>y</m>, <m>r</m>, and <m>\theta</m> in order for us to find the value of <m>y</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                What kinds of trigonometric functions connect these two side lengths of this triangle?
            </p>
        </hint>
    </task>
</activity>